# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. (.env.local is gitignored.)

# ── Clerk (auth) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get these from https://dashboard.clerk.com → your app → API Keys.
# Two instances, ONE userbase per instance:
#   • Development instance → test keys (pk_test_ / sk_test_)  → Local + Preview
#   • Production instance  → live keys (pk_live_ / sk_live_)  → Production
# The Production instance serves BOTH beta and stable users — the only
# difference is the `betaAccess` flag on publicMetadata (set on waitlist join;
# see lib/clerk/beta.ts). Use test keys here for local dev.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...

# After OAuth (Google) sign-in/sign-up, Clerk redirects here.
# The ?join=details param tells JoinContext to auto-open the details modal.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_AFTER_SIGN_IN_URL=/?join=details
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_AFTER_SIGN_UP_URL=/?join=details

# ── Supabase (database only — auth is handled by Clerk) ──────────────────────
# LOCAL DEV (recommended): run `supabase start` and use the local stack.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<printed by `supabase start` / `supabase status`>
# CLOUD: Project Settings → API for the hosted values below.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co

# Server-only — NEVER prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC. Bypasses RLS (service role).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Which Postgres schema to use (one instance, separated per environment):
#   public → Production + local dev   |   dev → Preview deploys
# Defaults to "public" when unset.
SUPABASE_DB_SCHEMA=public

# ── App URL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Google Analytics 4 (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measurement ID from https://analytics.google.com → Admin → Data streams.
# Leave UNSET locally: with no ID there is no tag, no cookie, and no consent
# banner (lib/analytics.ts). Set it in Vercel for Production only if you'd
# rather previews stay out of your reporting.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# ── Cloudflare Turnstile (bot check) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. `submitWaitlist` verifies this token server-side; it
# is what stops scripted multi-account referral farming (Clerk proves the email
# is real, but not that a human is driving).
#
# Leave both unset locally — with no secret and NODE_ENV!=production the check
# is skipped. In production a missing secret FAILS CLOSED (joins are rejected).
# To ship before Turnstile is set up, set TURNSTILE_DISABLED=true so that
# "no bot protection" is a deliberate, greppable decision rather than a silent
# misconfiguration — then remove it once the keys are in.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=your-turnstile-site-key
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=your-turnstile-secret-key
# TURNSTILE_DISABLED=true

# ── Payment (add when integrating Stripe / Polar / etc.) ─────────────────────
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...

# ── Admin console (/admin, /admin/beta, /admin/pipeline) ─────────────────────
# Who can see the admin surfaces. Comma-separated Clerk user ids
# (Clerk dashboard → Users → click yourself → copy the user_... id).
#
# FAILS CLOSED: if this is unset, every /admin route 404s for everyone,
# including you. That is deliberate — these surfaces carry every prospect's
# name, firm and compliance posture, every account's email address, and the
# button that grants beta access. None of that may be open whenever the
# deployment is misconfigured.
# ADMIN_USER_IDS=user_2abc...,user_2def...

# Where new team enquiries are announced. Configure either transport, or both.
#
# Strongly recommended before you start driving traffic to /for-teams. B2B
# enquiry conversion falls off a cliff with response time, and an email sitting
# unread for two days is a lost deal. These land on your phone.
#
# TELEGRAM. Needs a token AND a chat id, not a URL — Telegram has no "incoming
# webhook" like Slack does; you call sendMessage on a bot and tell it who to
# message. To get both:
#   1. Message @BotFather → /newbot → copy the token.
#   2. Send your new bot any message, then open
#      https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
#      and read result[0].message.chat.id  (personal chat = positive number,
#      group = negative). The bot cannot message you until you message it first.
# LEAD_NOTIFY_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:AA...
# LEAD_NOTIFY_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=123456789
#
# GENERIC WEBHOOK. Any endpoint accepting a JSON POST with a `text` field
# (Slack) or `content` (Discord); most automation tools take one or the other.
# LEAD_NOTIFY_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...

# Salt for the visitor-IP hash used to rate-limit the public lead form. We never
# store raw IPs — a privacy-first product that logged visitor addresses to
# protect a contact form would be contradicting itself. Any long random string.
# Falls back to SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY if unset, which works but means
# rotating that key silently resets the rate limiter.
# LEAD_IP_SALT=some-long-random-string

# ── Polar (payments — Merchant of Record) ────────────────────────────────────
# Decision record: docs/10-monetization.md. Polar is the MoR, so it owns global
# VAT/GST and invoicing and pays out as a single B2B relationship — which is why
# it beats Stripe for an Indian seller with worldwide customers.
#
# Organization Access Token: Polar dashboard → Settings → Developers.
# POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=polar_oat_...

# Webhook signing secret: Polar dashboard → Settings → Webhooks → your endpoint.
# The endpoint URL is https://your-domain/api/webhooks/polar
# Without this the webhook route throws at module load, which is deliberate — an
# unverified webhook endpoint is a "give me Pro for free" button.
# POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=polar_whs_...

# "sandbox" (default) or "production". Anything other than the literal string
# "production" is treated as sandbox, so a typo fails safe.
# POLAR_SERVER=sandbox

# Product ids, from Polar → Products. Checkout only offers a plan whose id is
# set here, so leaving these unset keeps /pricing saying "free during beta" and
# renders no Buy buttons — see selfServeCheckoutReady() in lib/billing/plans.ts.
# Self-serve:
# POLAR_PRODUCT_PRO_MONTHLY=...
# POLAR_PRODUCT_PRO_YEARLY=...
# POLAR_PRODUCT_LIFETIME=...
# Sold through a conversation at /for-teams, never self-serve — the Practice
# tier promises a signed DPA, which needs a human first:
# POLAR_PRODUCT_TEAM=...
# POLAR_PRODUCT_PRACTICE=...

# ── Eval history ingest (/eval) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret that protects POST /api/eval/ingest, the only way a run gets
# onto the public /eval page. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 24`.
#
# FAILS CLOSED: with this unset the route refuses every upload (503) rather than
# accepting unauthenticated ones. An open ingest on a page whose whole purpose
# is to be believable is a "publish any numbers you like" button.
#
# The pusher is `eval/text-cleanup/run-eval.sh` in the whisper-master repo; it
# reads the same value from its own environment.
# EVAL_INGEST_TOKEN=

# Optional, and only for the macOS sync routes: pins the `azp` claim on the
# Clerk session tokens /api/usage and /api/notes accept. Comma-separated.
# Unset is fine — the token is still cryptographically verified.
# CLERK_AUTHORIZED_PARTIES=
